  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-89",
              "name": "SQL Injection Authentication Bypass via Email Parameter in /rest/user/login",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SQL Injection Authentication Bypass via Email Parameter in /rest/user/login"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The login endpoint at `/rest/user/login` is vulnerable to SQL injection through the email parameter. An attacker can bypass authentication using boolean-based SQL injection (`' OR 1=1 --`), allowing login as any user (including admin) without knowing the password."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The login endpoint at `/rest/user/login` is vulnerable to SQL injection through the email parameter. An attacker can bypass authentication using boolean-based SQL injection (`' OR 1=1 --`), allowing login as any user (including admin) without knowing the password.\n\nComplete authentication bypass allowing unauthenticated attackers to gain admin access. Attackers can obtain valid JWT tokens for any user account, access all user data (including password hashes), and perform administrative actions without providing valid credentials.\n\n1. Use parameterized queries (prepared statements) for all database operations. Replace string interpolation with query parameters that prevent SQL syntax from being interpreted as part of the query. 2. Implement input validation and sanitization on all user-supplied inputs before using them in database queries. 3. Use an ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) framework that handles parameterization automatically. 4. Apply the principle of least privilege to database user accounts used by the application. 5. Implement rate limiting and account lockout mechanisms on the login endpoint to slow down brute force and injection attacks. 6. Add comprehensive logging and alerting for suspicious login attempts.",
                "markdown": "The login endpoint at `/rest/user/login` is vulnerable to SQL injection through the email parameter. An attacker can bypass authentication using boolean-based SQL injection (`' OR 1=1 --`), allowing login as any user (including admin) without knowing the password.\n\nComplete authentication bypass allowing unauthenticated attackers to gain admin access. Attackers can obtain valid JWT tokens for any user account, access all user data (including password hashes), and perform administrative actions without providing valid credentials.\n\n1. Use parameterized queries (prepared statements) for all database operations. Replace string interpolation with query parameters that prevent SQL syntax from being interpreted as part of the query. 2. Implement input validation and sanitization on all user-supplied inputs before using them in database queries. 3. Use an ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) framework that handles parameterization automatically. 4. Apply the principle of least privilege to database user accounts used by the application. 5. Implement rate limiting and account lockout mechanisms on the login endpoint to slow down brute force and injection attacks. 6. Add comprehensive logging and alerting for suspicious login attempts."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.8",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-89",
                  "stride:T"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "strix-coverage/sql-injection-search-parameter",
              "name": "SQL Injection / Search Parameter",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Coverage: SQL Injection / Search Parameter"
